# Level03

## Reconstructed source

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>

void decrypt(int key)
{
    char buffer[17];
    unsigned int i;

    buffer[0]  = 0x51; buffer[1]  = 0x7d; buffer[2]  = 0x7c; buffer[3]  = 0x75;
    buffer[4]  = 0x60; buffer[5]  = 0x73; buffer[6]  = 0x66; buffer[7]  = 0x67;
    buffer[8]  = 0x7e; buffer[9]  = 0x73; buffer[10] = 0x66; buffer[11] = 0x7b;
    buffer[12] = 0x7d; buffer[13] = 0x7c; buffer[14] = 0x61; buffer[15] = 0x33;
    buffer[16] = 0;

    for (i = 0; i < strlen(buffer); i++)
        buffer[i] ^= key;

    if (strcmp(buffer, "Congratulations!") == 0)
        system("/bin/sh");
    else
        puts("Invalid Password");
}

void test(unsigned int a, unsigned int b)
{
    unsigned int key;

    key = b - a;
    if (key <= 0x15)
        decrypt(key);
    else
        decrypt(rand());
}

int main(void)
{
    int number;

    srand(time(NULL));
    puts("***********************************");
    puts("*               level03         **");
    puts("***********************************");
    printf("Password:");
    scanf("%d", &number);
    test(number, 0x1337d00d);
    return (0);
}
```

## Vulnerability

There is no memory corruption here; the level is a reversing puzzle. The program
reads an integer and calls `test(number, 0x1337d00d)`. `test` computes
`key = 0x1337d00d - number` and, only if `key <= 0x15`, calls `decrypt(key)`
(otherwise it decrypts with a random key and fails).

`decrypt` XORs a fixed 16-byte blob with `key` and, if the result equals
`"Congratulations!"`, spawns a shell with `system("/bin/sh")`.

The weakness: the gate `key <= 0x15` leaves only **22 possible keys** (`0x15`
is `21`). The correct key can be found by trying all of them; no cryptanalysis
is required.

## Exploitation

Because `key = 0x1337d00d - number`, decrementing the input by 1 increases the
key by 1:

```
number = 0x1337d00d       -> key = 0
number = 0x1337d00d - 1   -> key = 1
...
number = 0x1337d00d - 21  -> key = 21
```

So sweeping `number` from `0x1337d00d` down to `0x1337d00d - 21` tries every
valid key. The program reports the outcome itself: `Invalid Password`, or a
shell. The shell appears at `0x1337d00d - 18`:

```
0x1337d00d = 322424845
322424845 - 18 = 322424827
```

```sh
echo 322424827 | ./level03
# Password: -> system("/bin/sh")
whoami                              # level04
cat /home/users/level04/.pass
```

(`0x1337d00d` in decimal is `322424845`; `python3 -c 'print(0x1337d00d)'`.)

